possible to extract  verbatim training instances from  language models  in the first place because language  models are memorizing things  if you prompt gpt2 to complete the  sentence  my address is first main street san  francisco california  it generates the correct zip code and  this kind of memorization  is good especially with things that have  no pattern attached to them  like unique identifiers person names  addresses  phone numbers pattern extraction is what  language models usually do  the majority of the model's weights  cluster data points to correspond to a  common pattern together  the spare weights if there are some  model the outliers the unique points  with no pattern that are left out of the  cluster  and memorized as they are and this  memorization is good to capture things  like  zip codes but become problematic when  things appear only once or twice during  training and get memorized  for this the authors define the bad type  of memorization  decay eidetic memorization in simple  terms it just means that the string  was k eidetic memorized if it occurred  k times in the training data and is  extractable from the language model  under this definition it is ok for the  model to memorize things occurring  thousands of times in the training data  but bad if k is small now we know that  memorization happens whether we want it  or not  because the model wants to minimize the  loss in the end  so if the model has enough weights left  to model the outliers and patternless  data points  memorization happens so how do the  authors exploit this memorization for  developing their  attack their pipeline is quite elegant  they take gpt 2 and generate 200 000  samples of text with gpt2  conditioned on all sorts of prefixes but  these generations are not necessarily  memorized pieces of text they could be  anything  so they sort and select the most likely  pieces to be memorized  text but the problem here is how to  decide whether text was memorized or not  one 